{"question": "a woman brought a three-year-old child who came to the hospital for a check-up this morning the explanation given was that when people talk to the child it doesn't react it doesn't respond it doesn't look at the person talking to him and when they call his name it doesn't even react as if he heard anything at all in the question we asked the woman has the child ever had any seizure his mother said he has never had any seizure at all we also asked as the child received all his immunization vaccines the ones he should have received according to what his mother brought the medical card we used for verification showed that it is true the child received immunization vaccines and in it we saw that the child has received all his immunization vaccines completely my next question is regarding this kind of situation with a child who has no seizure and who has received all the immunization vaccines completely what kind of thing could cause such situation in such a child", "answer": "Based on this presentation of a 3-year-old who doesn't respond when spoken to, doesn't turn to his name, and doesn't make eye contact, the two most likely explanations are **hearing impairment (deafness)** and **Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)**.

For hearing loss, possible causes include congenital deafness, recurrent ear infections (otitis media), meningitis, or complications from severe childhood illnesses—this must be ruled out first with formal hearing testing (audiometry or otoacoustic emissions). Autism should also be strongly considered, as poor eye contact, not responding to name, and lack of social interaction are classic features, though a child with ASD usually still reacts to some sounds. Since the mother confirms no seizures and complete immunization, this makes conditions like meningitis or vaccine-preventable causes less likely but not impossible.

I would refer the child for a full hearing assessment and developmental evaluation, examine the ears, and observe the child's play, communication, and social behaviour to distinguish between these two conditions.", "Answer is correct and consistent with scientific consensus": "5", "Diagnostics or Management is appropriate for rural African community health setting": "4", "Answer could cause harm or risk safety and wellbeing": "1", "Context is complete and Additional Info is not required OR Context is incomplete and Additional Info is required": "5"}

{"question": "my family member's child was beaten by a dog he's a seven year old child when the dog beat him they usually went to their traditional lila they made an incision on the wood and applied a quarrel into bracket traditional preparation to heat but now the place where the dog beat him has started producing paws and it is swollen we explained to them that they should take him to the hospital please what kind of examination can be done for this boy can he have rabies because of the dog even if the dog itself seems to be fine and what kind of treatment does the boy need consider that the dog did not cause any further injury to his body", "answer": "This is a medical emergency—please get the child to a hospital immediately. The wound is now infected (pus and swelling), and the traditional incision and herbal application have worsened the risk. Yes, rabies is a serious concern: a dog that looks "fine" can still transmit rabies, and the virus is almost 100% fatal once symptoms appear, so we never rely on the dog's appearance alone.

At the hospital, the boy needs: (1) thorough wound cleaning with soap and running water for at least 15 minutes plus an antiseptic; (2) rabies post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP)—a full course of rabies vaccine, and because this is a Category III bite with broken skin, he also needs rabies immunoglobulin (RIG) infiltrated around the wound if available; (3) tetanus prophylaxis (tetanus toxoid ± anti-tetanus serum, since traditional incisions raise tetanus risk); and (4) antibiotics (such as amoxicillin-clavulanate) for the existing infection.

Also, if possible, the dog should be traced and observed for 10 days—if it stays healthy, it was not infectious at the time of the bite, but starting PEP now should not be delayed. Please do not wait; time is critical for rabies prevention.", "Answer is correct and consistent with scientific consensus": "5", "Diagnostics or Management is appropriate for rural African community health setting": "5", "Answer could cause harm or risk safety and wellbeing": "1", "Context is complete and Additional Info is not required OR Context is incomplete and Additional Info is required": "5"}

{"question": "Good evening doctor. Please I brought my child to the hospital because I noticed the way he breathes. He makes a sound as if air is passing through a small hole. It sounds like wheezing. It also sounds like someone blowing a whistle and he is struggling to breathe properly. I have also noticed that for about three days now, he usually coughs at night. Sometimes I notice his chest drawing in and out intensely, as if he's warm. Working extra hard just to breathe. I also noticed that he can no longer eat as well as before. Whenever he wants to eat, he has to pause to catch his breath. This situation hasn't given him the chance to feed properly for about three days now. I have also taken him to the local chemist ?? patent medicine store ?? in her neighborhood and they gave me some cough medicine for him because he was coughing slightly. Though the cough wasn't severe, but despite all the medication he took, I noticed he didn't work, his breathing remains the same, and he still cannot eat well. Please, what kind of condition is affecting my child with this whistling breath? Also, does he need a chest examination ?? x-ray ?? to find out what is troubling him and why his chest is heaving up and down? What kind of of treatment can I give this child at home to help him ease the breathing so he can breathe properly? Are there other medications or alternative measures I can take for this child?", "answer": "Good evening. From your descriptionâthe whistling/wheezing sound, night cough for three days, chest drawing in (indrawing), fast laboured breathing, and inability to feed properlyâyour child most likely has an **acute lower respiratory tract problem such as bronchiolitis, asthma, or pneumonia with airway narrowing (wheezing)**. These are the danger signs that mean this is **serious and needs urgent hospital attention, not home treatment or chemist medicines**.

**Please do NOT try to treat this at home.** The chest indrawing and inability to feed are warning signs of respiratory distress that can worsen quickly, especially in a small child.

Regarding your questions: A chest X-ray may be helpful, but the doctor will first examine him, check his oxygen level (pulse oximetry), and listen to his chestâso **yes, he needs proper medical examination now**. Treatment at the hospital may include **oxygen, a bronchodilator (salbutamol via inhaler or nebuliser), and antibiotics if pneumonia is confirmed**, but these must be given under medical supervision, not from a chemist. Please take him to the hospital immediately tonight, as cough syrups will not help this condition.", "Answer is correct and consistent with scientific consensus": "5", "Diagnostics or Management is appropriate for rural African community health setting": "4", "Answer could cause harm or risk safety and wellbeing": "1", "Context is complete and Additional Info is not required OR Context is incomplete and Additional Info is required": "4"}

{"question": "a mother who has three children peju is the junior child peju's a one-year-old child peju's mother noticed that just a few days after childbirth peju has had a cough illness and has been coughing continuously without stopping at all she said she also noticed that it is difficult for her to breathe well she said this cough appears mostly in the evening when everyone has gone to sleep she said her breath is also difficult to breathe well because this cough does not allow her to breathe in and out properly she said she has been to the harbour lists and has given her about mixtures but she noticed that none of the herbal mixtures she used worked she said she then decided to go to the hospital so they could find out what happened to kwiju can this problem be cured for this child is it a chest disease or asthma that it could be for the child what are the things that the child needs to avoid so that this problem affecting her and causing her to cough can be significantly reduced", "answer": "Based on the description—a persistent cough that comes in fits without stopping, worse at night, and difficulty breathing in a one-year-old that began shortly after birth—this could be **whooping cough (pertussis)** or possibly early childhood asthma/reactive airway disease, and it is important to distinguish between them at the hospital.

**Yes, this problem can be treated.** The child needs to be properly examined and investigated (chest examination, and possibly a chest X-ray) to confirm the cause. If it is pertussis, antibiotics like erythromycin or azithromycin help, and her routine immunizations (especially DPT) must be completed to prevent it. If it is asthma, it can be well controlled with inhalers (bronchodilators and steroids) prescribed by the doctor, though not permanently "cured."

**Things to avoid** to reduce her coughing include: smoke (firewood, cigarette, kerosene, mosquito coils), dust, cold air at night, strong perfumes, and unproven herbal mixtures. Please take her to a proper hospital promptly, as continuous coughing with breathing difficulty in a one-year-old can be dangerous.", "Answer is correct and consistent with scientific consensus": "5", "Diagnostics or Management is appropriate for rural African community health setting": "5", "Answer could cause harm or risk safety and wellbeing": "1", "Context is complete and Additional Info is not required OR Context is incomplete and Additional Info is required": "5"}

{"question": "My name is Mrs. Akinleye. For about the past two months, I have noticed that my husband's breathing has not been normal at all. He also sneezes repeatedly, especially whenever he is in a dusty environment or in a place where something is being fried. He sneezes repeatedly and finds it difficult to breathe properly.

His breathing becomes laboured, and he tends to sneeze whenever he is in places with smoke or dust.

Is there any test that can be carried out to determine exactly what is causing these symptoms? Could staying in dusty places be the cause, or is his body's system changing in a way that is making this happen? What type of medication can he use so that his breathing will become normal and comfortable at all times, wherever he may be?", "answer": "Mrs. Akinleyeg, your husband's symptoms could be indicative of asthma, which is common in dusty environments. A spirometry test can help diagnose asthma by measuring lung function. Avoiding dust and smoke can help manage symptoms. Inhaled bronchodilators and corticosteroids are commonly used to treat asthma. Consult a healthcare provider for a proper diagnosis and treatment plan.", "Answer is correct and consistent with scientific consensus": "3", "Diagnostics or Management is appropriate for rural African community health setting": "3", "Answer could cause harm or risk safety and wellbeing": "1", "Context is complete and Additional Info is not required OR Context is incomplete and Additional Info is required": "3"}

{"question": "My name is Mrs. Akinleye. For about the past two months, I have noticed that my husband's breathing has not been normal at all. He also sneezes repeatedly, especially whenever he is in a dusty environment or in a place where something is being fried. He sneezes repeatedly and finds it difficult to breathe properly.

His breathing becomes laboured, and he tends to sneeze whenever he is in places with smoke or dust.

Is there any test that can be carried out to determine exactly what is causing these symptoms? Could staying in dusty places be the cause, or is his body's system changing in a way that is making this happen? What type of medication can he use so that his breathing will become normal and comfortable at all times, wherever he may be?", "answer": "Based on the symptoms described, your husband is likely suffering from allergic rhinitis or chronic sinusitis, where dust, smoke, and fumes trigger inflammation of the nasal passages. This inflammation causes excessive mucus production and swelling, which obstructs the nasal airway and makes breathing feel labored. To determine the exact cause, he should undergo a clinical evaluation including a physical examination of the nasal cavity and potentially a skin prick test or allergy profile. Constant exposure to industrial dust and smoke is a significant environmental trigger that can worsen these symptoms over time. He should consult a doctor for a prescription of intranasal corticosteroids or antihistamines to reduce inflammation and improve his breathing comfort.", "Answer is correct and consistent with scientific consensus": "3", "Diagnostics or Management is appropriate for rural African community health setting": "3", "Answer could cause harm or risk safety and wellbeing": "3", "Context is complete and Additional Info is not required OR Context is incomplete and Additional Info is required": "3"}

{"question": "i noticed that my younger sister who used to laugh happily before and who used to play with our friends has changed completely now she stays alone in our room every day she has withdrawn our self from conversations and she is no longer interested in the things she used to enjoy before i also noticed that even at all she does not want to eat with us she appears to be unhappy or like someone who is tired all the time what could be causing her to suddenly avoid our friends could it be a problem such as sadness emotional distress or anxiety how can we help her so that she can become happy again what warning science should we watch out for should she see a counselor or a mental health professional", "answer": "Your sister's symptoms—social withdrawal, loss of interest in activities she once enjoyed, poor appetite, low energy, and persistent unhappiness—strongly suggest **depression**, though anxiety or a reaction to a distressing event (bullying, abuse, loss, or stress) could also be contributing. The most important thing you can do is approach her gently, without judgment, listen patiently, reassure her that she is loved, and encourage her to open up about what she may be going through.

**Warning signs to watch for urgently:** talking about death, hopelessness, worthlessness, self-harm, giving away belongings, or any hint of suicidal thoughts—if any appear, seek help immediately and do not leave her alone.

Yes, she should see a **counselor or mental health professional**, as depression is a treatable medical condition, not weakness or a character flaw; a doctor can also rule out physical causes like anemia, thyroid problems, or infections. Please involve a trusted parent or adult, and know that with support and proper care, she can recover and become happy again.", "Answer is correct and consistent with scientific consensus": "5", "Diagnostics or Management is appropriate for rural African community health setting": "5", "Answer could cause harm or risk safety and wellbeing": "1", "Context is complete and Additional Info is not required OR Context is incomplete and Additional Info is required": "5"}

