{"question": "I'm 32 years old. I just gave birth. I just gave birth seven days ago. I noticed that if I'm breastfeeding the child, he struggles to suck the breast. If the child sucks a little bit, the child will remove his mouth. If I try to put the breast in his mouth so he can suck, he will suck again a little bit and remove his mouth. I now thought that why doesn't the child suck breasts well, I now notice. That the child has cold. He's not breathing out well, like a child snoring, that's how he sounds. I called the attention of my mother-in-law. She said maybe while we want to give birth to him, maybe water entered his head or other things. But I thought if water entered his head, the nurses and doctors ought to know if water entered the child's head. What now shattered my heart the most was the way he's not feeding well. It's given me concern and my mind is not at rest. He does not suck well at all. It's by force anytime I want to breastfeed the child. I have to wake him to suck a little. That's if he agrees to. What I want to ask is that what's the cause of the cold that he doesn't breathe well, and that any time he breathes, it will look like someone that the nose is blocked, and that what is the cause that he's not sucking breast.", "answer": "Congratulations on your baby, and it is completely understandable but very important that you are concerned.

The main reason your baby is struggling to breastfeed is because newborns are obligate nasal breathers, meaning they must breathe through their nose while sucking. When your baby's nose is blocked by mucus or a cold, they cannot breathe while feeding, forcing them to pull away from the breast to catch their breath. The "snoring" sound you hear is simply air struggling to pass through these congested nasal passages, which is a common physical issue and not caused by "water in the head."

However, because poor feeding and breathing difficulties in a one-week-old baby can quickly lead to dehydration or indicate a serious infection, please take your baby to a hospital immediately for a proper evaluation.", "Answer is correct and consistent with scientific consensus": "2", "Diagnostics or Management is appropriate for rural African community health setting": "2", "Answer could cause harm or risk safety and wellbeing": "4", "Context is complete and Additional Info is not required OR Context is incomplete and Additional Info is required": "2"}

