", "answer": "Given the history of an unvaccinated dog bite and the signs of swelling and redness, your child is at high risk for both bacterial infection (such as cellulitis or tetanus) and rabies. Since the dog's vaccination status is unknown, you must immediately initiate the Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) protocol, which includes the Rabies Vaccine and Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG) injected into the wound site. To manage the local infection and prevent tetanus, the wound must be thoroughly irrigated, cleaned with soap and water, and treated with a course of prophylactic antibiotics and a tetanus toxoid booster. We do not typically test the child for rabies, as the disease is fatal once symptoms appear; prevention through immediate vaccination is the only standard of care. Please ensure the child completes the full vaccine schedule as prescribed to ensure complete protection.", "Answer is correct and consistent with scientific consensus": "4", "Diagnostics or Management is appropriate for rural African community health setting": "4", "Answer could cause harm or risk safety and wellbeing": "1", "Context is complete and Additional Info is not required OR Context is incomplete and Additional Info is required": "4"}

